.TH SPLIT 1.SH NAMEsplit \- cut a file into pieces.SH SYNOPSIS\fBsplit\fR[\-l lines | \-b bytes[km] | \-C bytes[km] | \-lines] [infile [prefix]].SH DESCRIPTION\fBsplit\fRis GNU split, from the textutils.  It copies\fIinfile\fR,\fRor the standard input when it is missing or\fB\-\fR,\fRinto pieces of a thousand lines each.  The pieces are named\fIprefix\fRfollowed by\fBaa\fR,\fR\fBab\fR,\fR\fBac\fRand on; the prefix is\fBx\fRunless one is given..SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-l\fI n\fRpieces of\fIn\fRlines.\fB\-\fIn\fRmeans the same.  Long form\fB+lines=\fIn\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-b\fI n\fRpieces of\fIn\fRbytes, or kilobytes or megabytes with a\fBk\fRor\fBm\fRafter the number.  Long form\fB+bytes=\fIn\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-C\fI n\fRpieces of as many whole lines as fit in\fIn\fRbytes.  Long form\fB+line-bytes=\fIn\fR.\fR.SH EXAMPLES.nfsplit -l 5 notes part   partaa, partab, partac ...split -b 64k big        xaa, xab ... of 64 kilobytes eachcat part* > whole       the pieces back together.fi.SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fR.SH SEE ALSOdivide, head, cat